# Target C-II — one-site open-guard criterion for Type-I containers

**Date:** 2026-09-13  
**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; proof-only Type-I refinement.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.

This note composes the open-guard packing-saturation theorem with the audited
contact-wall shields for the two Type-I inactive-container geometries. It replaces the
vertex-cover requirement in the strict-diameter exchange by a one-site requirement.
It does not prove that the required site exists.

The implication chain is

```text
strict contact-wall safe region with area > a0 times isolate count
-> lattice packing of one more site
-> strictify in the open region
-> delete all assigned isolates and one nonisolated contact vertex
-> fewer contacts, same diameter if necessary
-> contradiction to contact-minimality.
```

## 1. Normalized source and container

Let `A` be the contact-minimal, then diameter-pair-minimal triangle-free global
minimizer in the canonical C-II normal form, with diameter `D>1`. Let `C` be one
macroscopic Type-I inactive container, internal or exterior, and let `Z` be all actual
contact-isolated source points assigned to `C`; write `z=|Z|`.

The audited Type-I container theorem supplies:

1. `C` is a positive-area convex subset of `int(conv A)`;
2. every source point outside `Z` lies outside `int(C)`; and
3. the contact-wall shield protects points in the container from every source point
   outside its interior.

For an internal container, let `V_C` be its cyclic actual unit-contact boundary
   vertices. Define the strict safe region

\[
 S_C^\circ:=\{x\in\operatorname{int}C:
                    |x-p|>1\quad(p\in V_C)\}.
\tag{1.1}
\]

For an exterior cap with closing chord `[u,v]` and actual unit-contact path vertices
`V_C`, define

\[
 S_C^\circ:=\{x\in\operatorname{int}C:
       \operatorname{dist}(x,[u,v])>1,
       \ |x-p|>1\quad(p\in V_C)\}.
\tag{1.2}
\]

These sets are open. The strict contact-wall audit shows that changing the weak guards
in the audited safe-area theorem to strict guards changes only finitely many circle
arcs and therefore does not change the area lower bounds.

## 2. Strict protection against the retained source

Fix any nonisolated positive-contact vertex `v` and put

\[
 R=A\setminus(Z\cup\{v\}).
\tag{2.1}
\]

The contact neighbor of `v` is not in `Z`, so `R` is nonempty. Then

\[
 \boxed{S_C^\circ\subseteq U_R}
\tag{2.2}
\]

where

\[
 U_R=\{x:1<|x-r|<D\text{ for every }r\in R\}.
\tag{2.3}
\]

### Proof

Take `x in S_C^circ` and `r in R`. The point `r` lies outside `int(C)`. If the segment
`xr` leaves `C` through an internal unit-contact boundary edge, the two-endpoint
crossing shield applied with `|x-r|<=1` forces one boundary endpoint to be at distance
at most one from `x`, contradicting the strict vertex guards. Exit through a boundary
vertex contradicts the same guards directly. In an exterior cap, exit through the
closing chord contradicts the strict distance-to-chord guard. Hence `|x-r|>1`.

Also `x in int(conv A)` and `r in conv A`, so `|x-r|<D`: an interior point of a convex
body of diameter `D` cannot be at distance `D` from another point of that body. Thus
`x in U_R`. \(\square\)

The argument retains all source points outside `Z`; no contact-isolated point is put in
the positive-core affine kernel.

## 3. One-site saturation theorem

Let `a_0=sqrt(3)/2`. If

\[
 \boxed{\operatorname{area}(S_C^\circ)>a_0 z,}
\tag{3.1}
\]

then the Type-I survivor is impossible.

### Proof

Translate the unit triangular lattice over a fundamental parallelogram. The mean
number of lattice points in `S_C^circ` is `area(S_C^circ)/a_0`, so (3.1) gives a
finite unit-separated set `B subset S_C^circ` with `|B|>=z+1`. Since `S_C^circ` is
open and lies in `int(conv A)`, every finite `B` has diameter strictly below `D`.

The dilation strictification lemma applied inside `S_C^circ` gives `B'` with the same
cardinality, all internal distances strictly greater than one, and diameter strictly
below `D`. By (2.2), every cross distance from `B'` to `R` is also strictly between
one and `D`.

Choose any subset `Y\subseteq B'` with `|Y|=z+1`, and set

\[
 A' = R\cup Y.
\]

Then `|A'|=|A|`, no new unit contact is created, and all contacts incident to `v` are
removed. If `diam A'<D`, global minimality is contradicted. If `diam A'=D`, `A'` is a
triangle-free global minimizer with fewer contacts, contradicting contact-minimality.
This is exactly the full-source open-guard theorem; the source points `Z` are replaced
one-for-one and are not discarded. \(\square\)

Thus every Type-I survivor satisfies the sharp one-site area ceiling

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{area}(S_C^\circ)\le a_0 z.}
\tag{3.2}
\]

This is stronger than the §165 diameter-cover condition, which requires enough sites to
delete a vertex cover of the complete diameter graph. Here the surviving diameter pair
may remain; contact-minimality supplies the contradiction.

## 4. Explicit residual inequalities

The audited contact-wall area bounds now have a direct source-level consequence.

### Internal chamber

If `m` is the number of unit boundary edges and

\[
 r_\partial={A_C\over a_0}-{m\over2}+1-z,
 \qquad
 \kappa_*={\pi\over3\sqrt3},
 \qquad
 C_*={1\over2}+{2\pi\over3\sqrt3},
\tag{4.1}
\]

then the strict safe-area lower bound and (3.2) give

\[
\boxed{r_\partial\le \kappa_*m+C_* .}
\tag{4.2}
\]

The inequality is a necessary condition for survival, not a lower bound on spare
capacity. In particular, a positive linear residual above the right side would close
the internal Type-I branch immediately.

### Exterior cap

If the closing chord has length `s`, the actual contact path has `ell` unit edges, and

\[
 r_C={A_C\over a_0}+{s+\ell\over2}+1-z-(\ell+1),
 \qquad
 \lambda_*={2\over\sqrt3}+{1\over2},
\tag{4.3}
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
 r_C\le \kappa_*\ell+\lambda_*s+{2\pi\over3\sqrt3}-{1\over2} .
}
\tag{4.4}
\]

Again this is a saturation barrier. It does not claim that the residual is positive or
that the area inequality is strict.

## 5. Exact surviving obligation

The one-site theorem removes the combinatorial diameter-cover cost from the Type-I
exchange, but it leaves a discrete realization problem:

> prove `area(S_C^circ)>a_0|Z|` for one actual macroscopic container, or construct a
> spare point by a finite exact-contact argument when equality holds.

The current force-hull residual identity only gives a nonnegative aggregate Oler
residual. It does not provide the strict inequality above. Equality can be approached
by perturbed near-triangular packings with no unit contacts, so area alone cannot be
silently upgraded to a strict spare-site conclusion.

No theorem-state file is changed. C-II and Level I remain open.
